# Next.js writes a React Server Component payload beside every page of a
# static export (index.txt, __next._tree.txt and friends, 68 files here).
# They exist so client-side navigation does not refetch whole HTML pages, and
# Next prefetches them on hover and in viewport, so a JavaScript-rendering
# crawler will request them.
#
# They are served as text/plain, they contain the page's content, and being
# plain text they cannot carry a <link rel="canonical">. That is the exact
# shape of Search Console's "Duplicate without user-selected canonical":
# duplicate content on a URL that declares no canonical.
#
# X-Robots-Tag rather than a robots.txt Disallow, deliberately. Disallow stops
# the crawl but leaves anything already indexed in place; noindex lets Google
# fetch the file, read the directive and drop it. Do not add a Disallow for
# these as well, or Google can no longer see this header.
/*.txt
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
